    "['mpls ldp router-id [parameter1] force', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp interface lo0.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp interface lo0.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ldp interface lo0.1\\nset protocols ldp router-id 1.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, we use `set protocols ldp` to configure LDP settings\\n2. `interface lo0.1` specifies the loopback interface (JunOS typically uses lo0.x for loopbacks)\\n3. `router-id 1.1.1.1` explicitly sets the router ID (the IP of Loopback1 in the Cisco config)\\n4. Juniper doesn\\'t need the \"force\" keyword as it will automatically use the specified router-id\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command was forcing LDP to use Loopback1\\'s IP as router-id, which translates to explicitly setting that IP as router-id in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp interface lo0.1'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols ldp router-id 1.1.1.1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'set protocols ldp router-id 1.1.1.1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols ldp interface lo0.1\\nset protocols ldp router-id 1.1.1.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Juniper, we use `set protocols ldp` to configure LDP settings\\n2. `interface lo0.1` specifies the loopback interface (JunOS typically uses lo0.x for loopbacks)\\n3. `router-id 1.1.1.1` explicitly sets the router ID (the IP of Loopback1 in the Cisco config)\\n4. Juniper doesn\\'t need the \"force\" keyword as it will automatically use the specified router-id\\n\\nNote: The Cisco command was forcing LDP to use Loopback1\\'s IP as router-id, which translates to explicitly setting that IP as router-id in Juniper.', 'trans_template': 'set protocols ldp router-id 1.1.1.1'}]]": 1,
